1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Hi, I'm [Your Name], and this is TripRescue —

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:09,000
a travel platform I built to solve a problem every traveler knows.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Your flight, your hotel, and your plans usually live in
five different apps and emails, completely disconnected.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
When something goes wrong — a delay, a missed connection —
nothing talks to anything else.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
TripRescue treats your whole trip as one connected system,

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,500
so when one piece breaks, it knows exactly what else is
affected, and what to do about it.

7
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Let me show you.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
First, a real account.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
I'll register with a name, email, and password —
nothing here is faked or pre-filled.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That request goes to our FastAPI backend, which checks the email
isn't already taken, hashes the password — we never store it
in plain text — and creates the account.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:03,500
The backend then issues a signed session token, which the
browser stores and attaches to every request from here on.

12
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:15,000
If I'd typed the wrong password on a login instead, the backend
would reject it with a clear, generic error — it doesn't reveal
whether the email even exists.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
This is a brand-new account, so there's nothing here yet —

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
and that's the honest state, not a placeholder. Let's create a trip.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The only thing I type by hand is the trip name,
origin, destination, and dates —

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:42,000
that's genuinely all this form asks for today. When I save,
the backend creates this trip and attaches it to my account
specifically —

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:52,000
my user ID comes from my session token, not from anything the
browser sends, so there's no way to fake ownership of a trip.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And there it is, showing up immediately on my dashboard.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Right now the trip has no itinerary at all, so let's
add the first real piece: a flight.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
I enter the airline, the confirmation number, the origin and
destination airport codes, and the schedule.

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
To be completely honest with you — this is manual entry
today, not a flight search.

22
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Hotels, attractions, and custom events aren't buildable through
the interface yet either;

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
that's genuinely the next thing on the roadmap, not something
I'm going to pretend already works.

24
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:42,000
What is real is everything that happens after I save: the backend
validates the airport codes, checks I actually own this trip,

25
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
and creates the flight and its matching booking record in
one atomic database transaction —

26
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:00,000
either both are saved, or neither is, even if something
fails halfway through.

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
To show you what a fully built-out, connected trip looks like,

28
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I'm going to switch to TripRescue's example account —
Aisha's Ladakh Expedition,

29
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
an eight-day trip with flights, a hotel, and activities
already in place.

30
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Same login flow, same real backend — this is just
a different, richer dataset.

31
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Here's the Timeline, day by day.

32
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Here's the Graph view — this is the actual dependency structure:
which booking depends on which, and how much buffer time
sits between them.

33
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The Map plots every located stop geographically. And Bookings
is every confirmation number in one place.

34
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,000
All four views are reading the exact same trip data —
there's no separate copy anywhere.

35
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
[SIMULATION 1 — Flight Delay, simulated disruption]

36
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Now let's break something on purpose.

37
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
I'm going to simulate a three-hour delay on the
Mumbai to Delhi flight.

38
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
For this demonstration, the disruption and the recovery
response are simulated,

39
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:19,000
so we can show how the product actually behaves during a travel
interruption — this isn't pulling live flight-status data.

40
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
But watch what happens next: that delay cascades through the
whole graph in real time —

41
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
the tight connection in Delhi is now at risk, and
everything downstream gets re-evaluated.

42
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:41,000
TripRescue then generates several ranked recovery options,
each with a real cost and time tradeoff.

43
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
I'll pick this one.

44
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:54,000
And the itinerary updates immediately — new times, new
risk levels, propagated everywhere.

45
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
[SIMULATION 2 — Hotel Cancellation, simulated disruption]

46
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Let's try a different kind of disruption — the hotel
in Leh cancels on us.

47
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Again, this is a simulated scenario, not a live
cancellation notice —

48
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
but everything downstream of it is computed for real.

49
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
The engine checks what this hotel was anchoring —
the activities booked around it —

50
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:28,000
and generates alternatives from its hotel catalogue, which, to
be clear, is demo data, not a live booking search.

51
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
I'll compare two options side by side.

52
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:40,000
This one keeps more of the existing plan intact,
so I'll apply it.

53
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Notice the trip's financial exposure and refund numbers
update too —

54
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:56,000
those are computed live from the actual booking terms,
not hardcoded.

55
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
[SIMULATION 3 — Persistence & Isolation, real, not simulated]

56
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Unlike the last two, nothing here is simulated —
this is just the real app under normal conditions.

57
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
I'll refresh the browser.

58
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,000
The recovered trip is still exactly as I left it —

59
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
that's coming fresh from the database on every load,
not a client-side cache.

60
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,000
I'll log out and log back in.

61
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Still there. Now watch this: I'll switch to a second,
completely separate account.

62
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:36,000
Aisha's trip isn't just hidden — it's not in this
account's list at all.

63
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
The backend enforces that at the database query level, using
the identity from my session token, not anything the browser
could tamper with.

64
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
So what's actually running underneath all of this?

65
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:51,000
The frontend is React and Vite, talking to a FastAPI
backend over a typed API layer.

66
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Every request goes through authentication, then an ownership
check, then real business logic —

67
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:05,000
the propagation, scoring, financial, and refund engines you
just saw — before anything touches the database through
SQLAlchemy.

68
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
For the database itself: locally, I've been running this
against a real PostgreSQL instance in Docker,

69
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
with Alembic managing every schema change — not
a hand-edited table.

70
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:26,000
In production, the backend is hosted on Render, and it's built
to run against Supabase's managed Postgres the same way —

71
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:34,000
that connection is fully configured and tested locally, though
I haven't pointed the live production deployment at it yet.

72
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
So: user click, React state change, API request, authentication,
ownership check, business logic, database transaction, response,
and the UI updates — every single time.

73
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Let's look at the finished picture.

74
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
This trip has a real flight I added myself, plus the
seeded hotel and activity bookings,

75
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,000
all shown consistently across Timeline, Graph, Map, and Bookings.

76
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
It survived two simulated disruptions and came out the other
side with an updated, still-accurate plan.

77
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
It's tied to one authenticated account, isolated from
every other user,

78
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:14,000
and it persists through a refresh or a full logout —
because it's genuinely stored in a database, not held
together by browser state.

79
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:22,000
One more view before we close: Risk Intelligence, which
continuously scores every leg of the trip for exposure —

80
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,000
that's the same engine that powers the disruption simulations
you just saw, just running proactively instead of reactively.

81
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:37,000
That's TripRescue: real authentication, a real database, real
disruption and recovery logic,

82
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:44,000
and a connected view of your entire trip instead of
five disconnected ones.

83
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Today it supports flights end-to-end, with hotels, activities,
and live provider search as the clear next step —

84
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:00,000
the architecture is already built to support them.
Thanks for watching.
